[[:db/retract 17592186314970 :question/answer-fields 17592186314695] [:db/retract 17592186167291 :question/answer-fields 17592186164960] [:db/retract 17592186167291 :question/answer-fields 17592186164972] [:db/retract 17592186167291 :question/answer-fields 17592186164966] {:db/id 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" "question-4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166963 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" :question/id #uuid "e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" :question/math-academy-id "q-340506" :question/problem "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b29a15f-c347-547f-840b-cae52a93cae3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b29a15f-c347-547f-840b-cae52a93cae3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b29a15f-c347-547f-840b-cae52a93cae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05e1e707-efff-528a-b5df-65f3a2dfbd42" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ebb288a-d478-5621-bc91-6939d6730dab" "answer-05e1e707-efff-528a-b5df-65f3a2dfbd42" "answer-ecf5f702-69d4-5d19-b5e6-ecc99227ab1a" "answer-18a59817-6dfb-5331-b664-4eed5ae288bd" "answer-e8357363-67ee-56a4-a4ee-a6c60f170c06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ebb288a-d478-5621-bc91-6939d6730dab" :answer/id #uuid "6ebb288a-d478-5621-bc91-6939d6730dab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05e1e707-efff-528a-b5df-65f3a2dfbd42" :answer/id #uuid "05e1e707-efff-528a-b5df-65f3a2dfbd42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecf5f702-69d4-5d19-b5e6-ecc99227ab1a" :answer/id #uuid "ecf5f702-69d4-5d19-b5e6-ecc99227ab1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18a59817-6dfb-5331-b664-4eed5ae288bd" :answer/id #uuid "18a59817-6dfb-5331-b664-4eed5ae288bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8357363-67ee-56a4-a4ee-a6c60f170c06" :answer/id #uuid "e8357363-67ee-56a4-a4ee-a6c60f170c06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" :question/id #uuid "4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340515" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-92e0b86f-b977-514f-9e9a-2b4ac1cbe0d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92e0b86f-b977-514f-9e9a-2b4ac1cbe0d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "92e0b86f-b977-514f-9e9a-2b4ac1cbe0d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c1c02e86-9fa1-5e41-9588-96d01350f6b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9a63d038-bac5-5072-83a9-d1a2575602e5" "answer-01205902-7ed5-5562-9748-9119a799b0e3" "answer-c1c02e86-9fa1-5e41-9588-96d01350f6b0" "answer-caa70edf-67db-5711-80d1-9a6c3409bbee" "answer-9053ae09-1967-50b2-bec7-285800590fb1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a63d038-bac5-5072-83a9-d1a2575602e5" :answer/id #uuid "9a63d038-bac5-5072-83a9-d1a2575602e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01205902-7ed5-5562-9748-9119a799b0e3" :answer/id #uuid "01205902-7ed5-5562-9748-9119a799b0e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1c02e86-9fa1-5e41-9588-96d01350f6b0" :answer/id #uuid "c1c02e86-9fa1-5e41-9588-96d01350f6b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caa70edf-67db-5711-80d1-9a6c3409bbee" :answer/id #uuid "caa70edf-67db-5711-80d1-9a6c3409bbee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9053ae09-1967-50b2-bec7-285800590fb1" :answer/id #uuid "9053ae09-1967-50b2-bec7-285800590fb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97" :question/id #uuid "0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-340679" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))+\\frac{1}{6}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1674a71f-884a-5a58-b12e-e9101bb4e574" "answer-field-db85f0d1-90c6-55e8-8218-c8897868d5ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1674a71f-884a-5a58-b12e-e9101bb4e574" :answer-field/id #uuid "1674a71f-884a-5a58-b12e-e9101bb4e574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-83094832-5120-528d-afe1-e8471b128d10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdc708f1-6571-5820-8c85-8be92dcce522" "answer-83094832-5120-528d-afe1-e8471b128d10" "answer-8ecad901-e457-5a7f-9933-d49a111a2f39" "answer-a32e9ce1-165d-5035-b70b-2b4a5c8fee66" "answer-c163715f-e3af-5949-bcaf-b925167883f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdc708f1-6571-5820-8c85-8be92dcce522" :answer/id #uuid "fdc708f1-6571-5820-8c85-8be92dcce522" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83094832-5120-528d-afe1-e8471b128d10" :answer/id #uuid "83094832-5120-528d-afe1-e8471b128d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ecad901-e457-5a7f-9933-d49a111a2f39" :answer/id #uuid "8ecad901-e457-5a7f-9933-d49a111a2f39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a32e9ce1-165d-5035-b70b-2b4a5c8fee66" :answer/id #uuid "a32e9ce1-165d-5035-b70b-2b4a5c8fee66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c163715f-e3af-5949-bcaf-b925167883f9" :answer/id #uuid "c163715f-e3af-5949-bcaf-b925167883f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db85f0d1-90c6-55e8-8218-c8897868d5ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "db85f0d1-90c6-55e8-8218-c8897868d5ec" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-69cbe4e6-0948-5e9f-9176-a7c91f104c4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd85ba26-4b7d-5d1f-bcc0-b9b5eeb786e0" "answer-69cbe4e6-0948-5e9f-9176-a7c91f104c4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd85ba26-4b7d-5d1f-bcc0-b9b5eeb786e0" :answer/id #uuid "cd85ba26-4b7d-5d1f-bcc0-b9b5eeb786e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69cbe4e6-0948-5e9f-9176-a7c91f104c4c" :answer/id #uuid "69cbe4e6-0948-5e9f-9176-a7c91f104c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314970 :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below." :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d41b053-3b5f-5c34-91f8-1d643d3b5746"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d41b053-3b5f-5c34-91f8-1d643d3b5746" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d41b053-3b5f-5c34-91f8-1d643d3b5746" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-f54e1f11-1b9d-5dcf-819b-ddcca88bb9fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e3bcbcb0-ba3d-57ed-97b9-85742d944618" "answer-f54e1f11-1b9d-5dcf-819b-ddcca88bb9fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3bcbcb0-ba3d-57ed-97b9-85742d944618" :answer/id #uuid "e3bcbcb0-ba3d-57ed-97b9-85742d944618" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f54e1f11-1b9d-5dcf-819b-ddcca88bb9fa" :answer/id #uuid "f54e1f11-1b9d-5dcf-819b-ddcca88bb9fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167291 :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+9x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" "answer-field-a2554085-77fe-5157-9ee0-fb327d6f9788" "answer-field-5a51accb-bbae-52f7-8c5d-2c3c83aae97b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" "answer-ffd7dac4-b5f5-51c0-9495-3c31b7bdebf2" "answer-c04eff61-d1a2-5922-831e-05e57e1cedbe" "answer-de6a0d2c-a56d-5bb1-9b1c-cd4e2312cdc5" "answer-7d216d93-03a2-52c7-8109-b053ea99a0b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer/id #uuid "5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffd7dac4-b5f5-51c0-9495-3c31b7bdebf2" :answer/id #uuid "ffd7dac4-b5f5-51c0-9495-3c31b7bdebf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c04eff61-d1a2-5922-831e-05e57e1cedbe" :answer/id #uuid "c04eff61-d1a2-5922-831e-05e57e1cedbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de6a0d2c-a56d-5bb1-9b1c-cd4e2312cdc5" :answer/id #uuid "de6a0d2c-a56d-5bb1-9b1c-cd4e2312cdc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d216d93-03a2-52c7-8109-b053ea99a0b7" :answer/id #uuid "7d216d93-03a2-52c7-8109-b053ea99a0b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2554085-77fe-5157-9ee0-fb327d6f9788" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2554085-77fe-5157-9ee0-fb327d6f9788" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" :answer-field/choices ["answer-734b3ad1-94c6-5ccc-a8b8-d77fb92327cb" "answer-f656d122-43f2-55e3-b008-141d9bf4c09e" "answer-b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" "answer-74949511-a7ef-5835-b1ed-11c9565189c6" "answer-281d8718-96cc-5786-9125-bdda8d8dfa5d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-734b3ad1-94c6-5ccc-a8b8-d77fb92327cb" :answer/id #uuid "734b3ad1-94c6-5ccc-a8b8-d77fb92327cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f656d122-43f2-55e3-b008-141d9bf4c09e" :answer/id #uuid "f656d122-43f2-55e3-b008-141d9bf4c09e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" :answer/id #uuid "b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74949511-a7ef-5835-b1ed-11c9565189c6" :answer/id #uuid "74949511-a7ef-5835-b1ed-11c9565189c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-281d8718-96cc-5786-9125-bdda8d8dfa5d" :answer/id #uuid "281d8718-96cc-5786-9125-bdda8d8dfa5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a51accb-bbae-52f7-8c5d-2c3c83aae97b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a51accb-bbae-52f7-8c5d-2c3c83aae97b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" "answer-4ef2209e-3d8f-5297-ad7a-edc869e85aa5" "answer-f2ac8e7f-277f-5ca0-83dd-f93b1ee8e219" "answer-88d440e0-29f7-5376-a757-92d36791c9cd" "answer-c052414d-62b2-5ddf-8d38-b8519a51bb8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" :answer/id #uuid "5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ef2209e-3d8f-5297-ad7a-edc869e85aa5" :answer/id #uuid "4ef2209e-3d8f-5297-ad7a-edc869e85aa5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2ac8e7f-277f-5ca0-83dd-f93b1ee8e219" :answer/id #uuid "f2ac8e7f-277f-5ca0-83dd-f93b1ee8e219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88d440e0-29f7-5376-a757-92d36791c9cd" :answer/id #uuid "88d440e0-29f7-5376-a757-92d36791c9cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c052414d-62b2-5ddf-8d38-b8519a51bb8d" :answer/id #uuid "c052414d-62b2-5ddf-8d38-b8519a51bb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}+9x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186314989 :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{1}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168052 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$  \n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p,$ then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:  \n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b).$\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2).$ Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x).$ For instance, consider the function $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ defined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x).$ The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)!$ But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x)$ if we were to consider the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)=2\\sin(x)$ from, say, $x \\in [0,2\\pi]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168043 :tutorial/content "Once again, recall that we are considering a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ We represent $f$ by its Fourier series\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\big).\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients, we use the inner product on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x), g(x)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe have already seen the derivation for the coefficient $a_0$ early in the lesson.\n\nNow, we derive the formula for $a_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\cos(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\cos(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=a_n\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\cos^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\cos(nx),\\cos(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we derive the formula for $b_n$ for $n\\ge 1{:}$\n\n- First, we take the inner product of both sides with $\\sin(nx){:}$\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\left\\langle \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big(a_k\\cos(kx)+b_k\\sin(kx)\\big),\\,\\sin(nx)\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Using linearity, we get\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n=\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} a_k\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle\n\\!+\\! \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} b_k\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\n- By orthogonality, $\\langle 1,\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0,$ $\\langle \\cos(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ for all $k,$ and $\\langle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166990 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
